#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111702} :activity/id #uuid "33a94a7b-379f-5b7b-bcb4-80cdb16140fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048789 :topic/id #uuid "bf1964cf-beba-5911-abd1-76470578e4aa" :topic/math-academy-id 1530} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111825 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=2^x$. First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y = 2^x$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e987a8647092c836bde4bcea31dc075e80e9da36a67133fc2b9431b99c5e42ed.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1)$.\n2. The graph is increasing.\n3. For $x>0$, the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ increases. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty$.\n4. For $x<0$, the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ decreases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has the horizontal asymptote $y=0$. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty$.\n5. The domain is $x\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$, and the range is $y>0$.\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $a>1$. These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/48/48f7bc7bb25bf1955d1e2cf48cffbf09e17f80a1228cada1c31f7ad4822bdace.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1a4d886d-d7b8-5667-b52a-2c2eb4a35b91" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1540 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "af86e00c-f654-5321-9355-dbe234a657e3" :step/math-academy-id 516446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110732 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110310 :question/id #uuid "99ab6141-164f-5f36-b55b-6e7fa1c8770c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1215"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf40e17e-0461-54a3-8f23-8424d694f189" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111335}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of an Exponential Growth Function"} :step/id #uuid "9c436809-f5cb-527a-907e-bd891aa0951c" :step/math-academy-id 516447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111704}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111704 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110733 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110311 :question/id #uuid "45473f44-206e-5eb6-a663-04d264e6a890" :question/math-academy-id "e-1216"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6fa9e7b-172d-57b6-8b6e-0db0f8f802ad" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111334}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements about Exponential Growth Functions"} :step/id #uuid "de707c0a-c932-5251-81f5-798ae37e792e" :step/math-academy-id 516450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111705}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111826 :tutorial/content "Multiplying an exponential growth function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve $y=2(3^x)$ represents a vertical scaling of $y=3^x$ by a factor of $2$. It takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y=3^x$ and multiplies them by $2$. This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/49/49c2136298e6fbf1adcc4c24ddef6d3daf47367d4d3f15dc18c476dff086087a.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "8c1fa510-e5f3-585b-852a-c46724144ec1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1541 :tutorial/title "Vertical Stretching"} :step/id #uuid "82779645-e04b-59c6-a05e-c042063885a7" :step/math-academy-id 516453 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111706}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110369 :question/id #uuid "e5680653-d529-58df-b243-7447db0a750a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1802"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7654997-ee9a-5d6c-88e9-d46f36bd8e88" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111399}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Stretched Exponential Growth Function"} :step/id #uuid "4b8c5352-dd55-52e0-83dd-102169a1a723" :step/math-academy-id 516454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111707}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111707 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111833 :tutorial/content "The curve\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{3}(3^x)\n$$\n\nrepresents a vertical scaling of $y=3^x$ by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nIt takes all of the $y$-coordinates on $y=3^x$ and multiplies them by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThis has the effect of squashing, or **compressing**, the curve downwards.\n\n![](images/e3/e3ae2f177dd60050dd4c4035c66ebfc46d1028eed74f1d23178ddaae35d35378.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remained the same, and the $y$-intercept moved from $(0,1)$ to\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nIn general for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve $y=k(a^x)$,\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ remains the same, and\n- the $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "78c17e05-8ef3-58fd-a9ea-a2fc04f4bd83" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1858 :tutorial/title "Vertical Compression"} :step/id #uuid "254e2522-a3c6-57f8-9ac1-0e813acdc6d7" :step/math-academy-id 516458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111708}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110312 :question/id #uuid "3661675e-e7da-5834-97cf-017e4e084275" :question/math-academy-id "e-1217"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8a6ac29-44ee-5305-be7a-41f9ae742756" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111100}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Compressed Exponential Growth Function"} :step/id #uuid "fbd6d68b-40b3-5cb7-afa4-487f4a8e9f03" :step/math-academy-id 516459}] :activity/title "Graphing Exponential Growth Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110875}]}
